      <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>
During the blower door test, we identified that your house has some excess air leakage associated with the following locations:

** Some around the crawl access panel in the floor
** The entrance doors - these doors are not sealing properly and likely require new weatherstripping
** The bathroom exhaust fans - It appears the exhaust fans dampers are not sealing well and could be replaced with the new ENERGY STAR* rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit
** Some of the electrical outlets in walls &amp; around heat registered in the floor of the section of house that is directly above the crawlspace
** The current 6" combustion air supply pipe for the furnace (and the woodstove)

Savings: I modeled a modest 10% reduction in air leakage, that is estimated to save $ 208 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 6.4 GJ. There are currently no rebates through the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for air sealing.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $550 and $1000 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake. An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.

</airSeal>
                <windows>
As you may be interested in upgrading a few windows, I modeled an upgrade to the 2 x older skylights - to make sure this upgrade recommendation is in your file in case you decide to apply to the Canada Greener Homes Program !!

There are 2 x energy efficiency rebate levels provided through the Canada Greener Homes Program (see the information below).

Savings: By replacing the 2 x skylights with ones that meet the minimum energy efficiency criteria of the Canada Greener Homes Program, you could potentially save $ 40 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 1.2 GJ. There are currently no rebates through the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for upgrading windows.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $125 or $250 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the Searchable product list of eligible windows for the Canada Greener Homes Grant.

ENERGY STAR labels must be affixed to windows at the time of installation and remain in place until the time of your post-retrofit evaluation. If ENERGY STAR labels are removed prior to the post-retrofit evaluation, the following photos are your responsibility to acquire, retain and provide to the energy advisor at the time of the post-retrofit evaluation:
A photo of each newly-installed window showing a portion of the surrounding wall assembly from the inside, outside or both, AND

A photo of each ENERGY STAR label affixed to the window that clearly shows the ENERGY STAR certification mark, Manufacturer model code/number, U-factor/Energy Rating, NRCan reference number or a Certified Products Directory (CPD) number, and a Certified mark from one of the following:
? Canadian Standards Association (CSA)
? Intertek Canada
? Keystone Certifications
? Labtest Certification (LC)
? QAI Laboratories
? National Fenestration Council (NFRC)

Removed labels must be submitted and provided to the energy advisor.

Request your supplier/installer to include the following information on the invoice:
? Name of the homeowner and address of the house;
? Date of installation;
? Window make and model numbers;
? NRCan Reference Number;
? Indication of whether windows are ENERGY STAR or ENERGY STAR Most Efficient certified;
? Number of windows and location of each installation;
? U-factor/Energy Rating (ER);
? Itemized cost of each window and total cost of installation; and
? Itemization of any other work done as part of the installation, such as air/gap sealing and associated costs.

Please note that a window schedule (quote) cannot be used as a substitute for the aforementioned acceptable window validations and cannot be submitted to the portal as a standalone document to validate window installation and eligibility. However, the window schedule may be attached to the invoice provided there is a reference on the invoice to a schedule reference number to link the two documents.</windows>
                <doors>
As you may be interested in upgrading a few doors, I modeled an upgrade to just the front door for now - to make sure this upgrade recommendation is in your file in case you decide to apply to the Canada Greener Homes Program !!

Savings: By replacing the front door with one that meets the Canada Greener Homes energy efficiency criteria (see the information below), you could potentially save $ 5.50 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 0.2 GJ. There are currently no rebates through the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for upgrading doors.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $125 per hinged door system is available when an eligible ENERGY STAR certified door or door system is installed. Replacing old, damaged or leaky hinged doors with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy and improve comfort. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>
Your main house and the secondary suite each currently have a 182 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. You may want to consider replacing these electric hot water heaters with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heaters with an energy factor of 0.95 or higher.

Savings: By replacing the 2 current electric hot water tanks with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heaters with a thermal efficiency of 0.95, could potentially save $ 1,526 per year in energy costs, slightly decrease your annual energy usage by 0.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 ($750 x 2) in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>
Your main house is currently heated with a 83% efficient oil furnace. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 96% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.

Savings: By replacing your current propane furnace with a 96% AFUE condensing propane furnace with an ECM fan blower, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 1,233 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 26.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,600 is available for replacing an oil-fired furnace with an ENERGY STAR certified propane-fired furnace that is a minimum 97% AFUE. To determine the eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that the unit is included on the Searchable product list of eligible furnaces or boilers for the Canada Greener Homes Grant.

Note: You can still get GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate if you were to install 96% AFUE condensing furnace but you will not be eligible for Canada Greener Homes Grant.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

BIOMASS ALTERNATE HEATING OPTIONS

1. Your main house has a wood stove (Pacific Energy Super 27) that has a Higher Heating Value (HHV) efficiency rating of 71.3%. It vents up a 6" chimney and appears to draw its' combustion air from inside the house (ie: no outside combustion air supply directly connected to the wood stove - so it is likely sharing the combustion air provided by the 6" pipe in the furnace room). If you were to use this wood stove to cover about 75% of the annual heating requirements of the main floor of your main house (and of course, heat will rise to the 2nd floor due to your vaulted main floor design):
    A) You could potentially save $ 566 per year in net energy costs, if you purchased wood @ $420/cord
    B) You could potentially save $ 1,320 per year in net energy costs, if you self-harvested at a nominal @ $100/cord

2. You wondered about switching to a Pellet Stove. If you replaced the wood stove with an 82% efficient wood pellet stove (that has a dedicated &amp; sealed outside combustion air supply), and used it to cover about 75% of the annual heating requirements of the main floor of your main house (and of course, heat will rise to the 2nd floor due to your vaulted main floor design):
   A) You could potentially save $ 741 per year in net energy costs, if you purchased wood pellets at @ $6.00/bag.

An eligible wood pellet stove can qualify for a 50% rebate of the purchase cost up to $1,000  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>
Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely with the short and dark days of winter. Although winter may provide little or no usable energy (depending on your latitude in the NWT), the long hours of sunlight in spring, summer and fall can provide significant solar heating and electricity generation opportunities for up to eight months of the year.  
As Solar Photovoltaic (PV) system prices continue to drop, they become more competitive with the cost of grid-supplied and especially off-grid generation.

Savings: By installing a 4 kW solar photovoltaic system on the front facing roof of your house (orientation = SE), you could potentially save $ 1,331 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 14.8 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program for electricity generation systems installed in a Hydro Community, including Yellowknife.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Photovoltaic Solar Panels: Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,300 per kW DC is available for installing a solar photovoltaic (PV) system. If you choose to install a PV system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar PV installer, including the measurement of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. 

The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW DC. The system must be composed of PV panels and an inverter certified to CSA Standards. Solar panels can be mounted on the house or ground, as long as they are on the property / land of the house associated with the application. Building permits may be required for both off-grid and grid-connected installations. For more information, refer to Natural Resources CanadaÂs Solar Ready Guidelines document that can be found online.</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>
CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.

Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.

ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION

For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.

Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:
- In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report
- If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.
- Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit
- All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional
- It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:
--- installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*
--- an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards
--- the heat pump has been sized to your entire home
*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.
- All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.

ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES

You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.
Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights Â Hydro Community

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 
To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test = Pass

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (bathroom fans, range hood and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 4.0 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace and wood stove are both chimney vented and are susceptible to back drafting.

During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed and operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house: 
        - kitchen range hood in the main house
        - bathroom fans in the main house
        - a clothes dryer in the main house (the dryer in the secondary suite was not yet vented out)
        - the main house furnace has a makeup outside air supply pipe to bring in outside fresh air each time one of the
          above exhaust fans is operating.
        - the secondary suite has an Energy Star HRV to provide balanced ventilation to that living space

You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fuel Costs Â Yellowknife

The following Dec. 1, 2021 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh
Oil - $1.25 per liter 
Propane - $0.73 per liter delivered
Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested
Pellets - $6.00 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Disclaimer
The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Your Energy Advisor

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.

Sincerely,

Michael Goodwin 
Senior Energy Management Specialist
Arctic Energy Alliance
Phone: (867) 920-3333</AdditionalComments>
